 "result": "The session cookie is written in `finalize_reply`, near the end of response finalization. In call order:\n\n1. `Tessel.run_full_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:992`) dispatches the view. It calls `run_view` and, on an exception, `route_user_fault`. It then calls `finalize_request`.\n2. `Tessel.finalize_request` (`src/tessel/app.py:1021`) turns the view's return value into a response with `make_response`. It then calls `finalize_reply`.\n3. `Tessel.finalize_reply` (`src/tessel/app.py:1394`) runs the `after_request` callbacks. If the session isn't a null session, it calls `session_interface.persist_session(...)` at line 1416.\n4. `SignedCookieSessionInterface.persist_session` (`src/tessel/sessions.py:337`) does the writing:\n   - It adds `Vary: Cookie` if the session was accessed.\n   - If the session is empty and was modified, it calls `response.delete_cookie`.\n   - Otherwise, if `cookie_needs_write` is true, it signs the session data with `make_signer(app).dumps(...)` and calls `response.set_cookie(...)` at line 374.\n\nI didn't trace the caller of `run_full_request`. That should be the WSGI entry point, `__call__` at `app.py:1618`.\n\nThe test client has its own path in `src/tessel/testing.py:165-177`. It calls `restore_session` and `persist_session` directly.",
